"""English KB part 9: boredom, apathy and melancholy (batch)."""

TOPICS = {
    "boredom": "Boredom is the state of being bored, the dull and flat wearying of the mind for want of a thing to engage it, and the tedious while endured with nothing that stirs the mind to wonder or to work; and it is the boredom of a long sermon heard with the eyes upon the glass, of an idle afternoon in a house that offers no errand, and of a task so often repeated that the hands perform it while the mind is elsewhere, being the dull and the unstirred estate; and it differs from apathy, which is the deadness of the affections, in being the fretting of the mind for want of occupation rather than the extinction of its feeling, and from weariness and from lassitude, which are the sinking of the strength under toil, in being the emptiness of the mind that would fain be stirred, and from melancholy, which is a dark and settled sadness, in being a flatness that seeks a cause rather than a gloom that has found one; and it is the boredom which, whether in a schoolroom or a long watch, is at once a tedium and a craving, being the dull and the seeking state; and a person is in boredom when the hours lie heavy and nothing presents itself worth the seizing, being the flat and the irksome one; and boredom is thus the state of being bored, the dull wearying of the mind for want of an engaging thing, and the hollow while endured with nothing to stir it.",
    "apathy": "Apathy is the state of being apathetic, the dead and unfeeling indifference of the mind toward things that ought to move it, and the cold stillness in which a person neither hopes nor fears nor desires, having ceased to be stirred by the hurts that once concerned him; and it is the apathy of a household that ceases to grieve for a grief too often seen, of a citizen who hears of public miseries without a stir, and of a soul that has long expected nothing and so is visited by no disappointment, being the cold and unfeeling estate; and it differs from boredom, which is the irked and empty mind seeking a thing to stir it, in being the settled stillness of a mind that no longer seeks, and from melancholy, which is a dark and brooding sadness, in being the absence of the feeling rather than its heaviness, and from weariness and from lassitude, which are the sinking of the body and the will, in being the extinction of the interest itself; and it is the apathy which, whether in a man, a crowd or a whole age, is at once a peace and a peril, being the cold state; and a person shows apathy when he remains unmoved by what would once have moved him, being the unconcerned one; and apathy is thus the state of being apathetic, the dead indifference of the mind toward things that ought to move it, and the cold stillness in which a person neither hopes nor fears nor desires.",
    "weariness": "Weariness is the state of being weary, the sinking of the frame and the spirits under long toil or the watching of many sorrowful hours, and the heavy estate in which every motion is an effort and every thought a labour slowly borne; and it is the weariness of a labourer at the fall of the evening, of a traveller upon a road that does not end, and of a mourner who has kept a long vigil, being the sinking estate; and it differs from lassitude, which is a slackness of the body more than of the will, in being the heavier and the more general sinking of both frame and spirit, and from exhaustion, which is the utter end of the reserve after a long and heavy demand, in being the sinking before the reserve is quite spent, and from boredom and from apathy in being the draining of strength rather than the emptiness of interest, and from melancholy, which darkens the mind, in being a heaviness of the limbs rather than a darkening of the soul; and it is the weariness which, when the day is long and the road is hard, is at once an honesty and a pleading, being the heavy state; and a person shows weariness when his limbs and his spirits flag and he would fain lay down his burden, being the drooping one; and weariness is thus the state of being weary, the sinking of the frame and the spirits under long toil, and the heavy estate in which every motion is an effort.",
    "melancholy": "Melancholy is the state of being melancholy, the settled and brooding sadness of the mind which no present cause explains, the dark humour in which a person dwells upon losses and upon the vanity of the world, and the sorrowful gravity that hangs over the spirit like a low cloud; and it is the melancholy of an autumn evening when the leaves fall, of a house emptied by death, and of a poet who finds a music in his own sadness, being the brooding estate; and it differs from apathy, which is the dead and unfeeling stillness of the mind, in being a sadness that feels too much rather than too little, and from boredom, which is a flat and irked emptiness, in being a settled gloom that has found its cause, and from weariness and from lassitude, which are the sinking of the strength, in being a heaviness of the soul rather than of the limbs, and from a mere solemnity, which is the gravity of a ceremony, in being the settled and personal gloom of the affections; and it is the melancholy which, whether in a man or a season, is at once a sorrow and a depth, being the brooding state; and a person is in melancholy when his spirit dwells long upon what is lost or passing, being the sorrowful one; and melancholy is thus the state of being melancholy, the settled sadness of the mind which no present cause explains, and the sorrowful gravity that hangs over the spirit like a low cloud.",
    "lassitude": "Lassitude is the state of being languid, the slack and sinking weakness of the body and of the will which follows upon a burning climate, a slow disorder or a long and unremitting strain, and the soft heaviness in which a person has not the heart to begin that which he knows he should begin; and it is the lassitude of a traveller in the heat of a tropic noon, of a convalescent whose strength comes back too slowly, and of a clerk after many months of a punctual and unvarying office, being the slack estate; and it differs from weariness, which is the heavier and the more general sinking of frame and spirit under toil, in being the softer languor which follows the heavier toil, and from exhaustion, which is the utter end of the reserve, in being a languor still capable of motion but without the will to it, and from apathy and from boredom in being a weakness of the body rather than a deadness of the affections or an emptiness of the interest; and it is the lassitude which, whether after a fever or after a summer, is at once a languor and a yielding, being the slack state; and a person shows lassitude when his limbs are heavy and his will is faint and he postpones what he ought to do, being the listless one; and lassitude is thus the state of being languid, the slack weakness of the body and of the will, and the soft heaviness in which a person has not the heart to begin.",
}

